module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.a_red_control_that_was_never_red_on_any_tree_reads_as_a_control

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { NoRungStated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data a_red_control_that_was_never_red_on_any_tree_reads_as_a_control: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "a_red_control_that_was_never_red_on_any_tree_reads_as_a_control" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: NoRungStated },

  receipts: [
    "**a red control that was never red on any tree reads as a control** (INVALID STATE: a roadmap row's red_control names claims that exist and that PASS on BOTH sides of the change the row asks for, and beside them a sentence promising an instrument -- 'an instrument shows no subtree re-walk on that path' -- while no counter for that instrument exists anywhere in the corpus. HARM: the row reads closed. A reader greps the named module, finds green claims, and cannot tell that they certify invariance ACROSS the change rather than sensitivity TO it; the promise beside them supplies the half the claims do not, and the two halves together look like a control. A control never observed red on any tree is not a control, which is why gunbc.design_ruling::executed_red_control_names_its_failing_commit makes red_at data rather than prose).",

    "SPECIMEN (2026-11-25, planning eager-bear-102, node v1-parse-allocator-carried, identity rn_BS0YK7QAA67P73F6YYGZRTGXJ3). At main 5611ac47e730 its red_control read: 'Allocated ids are identical before and after on a fixture with a long operator chain, and an instrument shows no subtree re-walk on that path.' The first clause is met by eight claims in src/v1/tests/claim/parse_allocator_postcondition_test.dag (test fns at lines 106, 117, 158, 167, 177, 282, 290, 298 of a 304-line module). The second has no witness: a case-insensitive grep of that module for rewalk, re-walk, instrument, occurrence_allocator_after, eval_steps or counter returns 0 matches, and occurrence_allocator_after_node has 0 hits in any claim module at main against 14 hits in src/v1/02_parse.dag. The eight claims are byte-identical between main and the implementation head 9eab2a2882a5 (module sha256 eb47ade80d3ba039ec4d4c551af3b841963a304ec4cb88b85610d49640e7dfdf, git diff --stat prints nothing) and all eight PASS at both trees, so none was ever red with respect to the deletion. The module's own header says the goldens were captured with the recovery walks present and that the two runs produced byte-identical digest lines: they certify allocator-identity invariance, which that same row puts in out_of_scope.",

    "MEASURED THE SAME DAY, so this row is not a claim that nothing was saved. The deletion is real (src/v1/02_parse.dag 7132 lines to 7006, occurrence_allocator_after_node 14 hits to 0, nine functions gone) and the saving is real and chain-shaped. One released claim_batch whose sha256 is a53867a57b587969e494b3989708ba741da2ad4ee7f316fb2a561a819e3afc6d (a binary hash, not a commit), one command line, only the tree varying, srv1 under heavy-slot, trees checked out clean at 5611ac47e730427f6d19f9bf765c561b01c36de7 and 9eab2a2882a56571220b0d0b5d8d7864b7d8aafd: chain200 identity eval_steps 418120 to 396027, chain200 content association 640998 to 618905, chain40 identity 88689 to 84196, chain40 content association 101218 to 96725, folds identity 48536 to 44896, folds content association 52937 to 49297. Every delta tracks chain length -- -22093 at length 200 and -4493 at length 40, a ratio of 4.9 for a 5x chain, which is LINEAR at these sizes and not the quadratic the row's displaced_cost asserts; that gap is left to the row's author rather than rewritten here. WHAT THAT TABLE IS NOT: a measurement with no verdict cannot gate. No claim asserted those numbers, nothing went red on them, and a differential quoted into prose is review evidence, not a control.",

    "WHAT CLOSES IT, AND WHY IT IS NOT WRITTEN HERE. The discriminating control available today is a step BUDGET: the row now carries B = 407000 eval steps on the chain-200 identity claim, with the pair above as its provenance, so the wrong case is refused (418120 over B) and the right case accepted (396027 under B) by the same reading the floor already uses for budgets (src/v2/workflow/required_floor.dag claim_eval_step_standing, EvalStepsOverBudget over EvalStepsWithinBudget). A budget refuses on COST and cannot name a CAUSE. The claim that would name the cause asserts a ZERO count of recovery walks on the left-operand path, and a zero-count claim needs a COUNTER: no counter over occurrence_allocator_after_node exists in the emitted host or in any claim module, and building one is implementation, so it belongs to a future node and not to a row edit. TRIGGER for this class: the re-walk returning on the left-operand path -- a producer stops publishing its minted identities, or the carried allocator is dropped and a recovery scan is re-added. The budget sees that only as a cost increase, and only if the fixture reaches the path; the bound is also crossable by legitimate work, whose remedy is a fresh pair measured at two named shas on a named binary, never a silent widening of the literal.",

    "RECOGNITION RULE. When a red_control names an instrument, grep the corpus for the counter it would read: 0 hits means the sentence is prose. Then ask whether the named claims were ever RED. Byte-identical at both trees and green at both means they certify invariance, not sensitivity -- the change could be reverted and every one of them would still pass. A row whose control cannot go red is an open row, whatever its fields assert.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
